# 5. FEMA compounding
write_page("fema-compounding-foreign-subsidiary-india", page(
    slug="fema-compounding-foreign-subsidiary-india",
    title="FEMA Compounding + Regularisation 2026 | Foreign Subsidiary India - BQP",
    description="FEMA voluntary compounding for foreign-shareholder Indian subsidiaries: regularise missed FC-GPR / FC-TRS / FLA / APR / Form ODI / Form FC-TRS filings, pricing guideline breach, sector-cap violation. Section 13 FEMA Act. Compounding application + hearing + order. Timeline 90-180 days. Working CA guide by Durgesh Chavda.",
    keywords="FEMA compounding India, FEMA voluntary compounding foreign company, FC-GPR late filing compounding, FEMA Section 13 penalty, regularise FEMA breach Indian subsidiary",
    hero_kicker="/ Foreign Clients &middot; FEMA Compounding &middot; Updated 2026-10-14",
    hero_title_html="FEMA compounding, <em>regularise before scrutiny finds it.</em>",
    hero_lead="Indian subsidiary of foreign parent discovers historical FEMA non-compliance &mdash; missed FC-GPR, late FLA Return, pricing guideline breach, incorrect Form FC-TRS, missed APR for ODI. Section 13 FEMA Act permits <strong>voluntary compounding</strong>: pay a defined penalty, settle the breach, close the exposure. Materially cheaper than being caught in audit or scrutiny. 90-180 day timeline. Here's the complete playbook.",
    sections=[
        ("/ Common FEMA breaches foreign-subsidiary carries", "What needs compounding.",
         "<ul>"
         "<li><strong>FC-GPR not filed / late filed</strong>: 30-day window from receipt of inward FDI remittance. Common breach when funds come in but filing is forgotten / delayed.</li>"
         "<li><strong>FC-TRS not filed / late filed</strong>: transfer of shares between resident and non-resident within 60 days. Missed when secondary share transfers happen.</li>"
         "<li><strong>FLA Return not filed</strong>: annual filing by 15 July (see our <a href='fla-return-foreign-liabilities-assets-rbi-india.html'>FLA guide</a>). Multi-year non-filing common.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Form ODI Part II (APR - Annual Performance Report) not filed</strong>: for Indian company's overseas investment. Annual by 31 December.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Pricing guideline breach</strong>: FDI issue / FC-TRS done at price below RBI pricing guidelines (DCF for unlisted) or above (for FC-TRS to non-resident).</li>"
         "<li><strong>Sector-cap breach</strong>: foreign investment exceeded sector FDI cap (approval route sector but handled as automatic).</li>"
         "<li><strong>Delay in share allotment post inward remittance</strong>: shares must be allotted within 60 days (previously 180 days). Delays trigger compounding.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Reporting delays on External Commercial Borrowings (ECB)</strong>: Form ECB / ECB-2 monthly reports.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Press Note 3 breach</strong>: investment from land-bordering country without prior approval.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Section 10 of FEMA</strong>: AD-category banking violations.</li>"
         "</ul>"),
        ("/ The compounding mechanism", "Section 13 FEMA Act.",
         "<p>Section 13 of FEMA Act 1999 + FEMA (Compounding Proceedings) Rules 2000 permit voluntary compounding of contraventions. Key features:</p>"
         "<ul>"
         "<li><strong>Voluntary application</strong>: filed by the contravener (Indian subsidiary / Indian branch / Indian individual) to RBI's Compounding Authority (Chief General Manager or Regional Director).</li>"
         "<li><strong>Admission of contravention</strong>: application must contain full disclosure of the breach + supporting documents.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Compounding fee</strong>: as determined by Compounding Authority. Based on guidelines + amount involved + nature of breach + delay.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Timeline</strong>: 180 days from acknowledgement of application &mdash; typical 90-180 days.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Hearing</strong>: usually physical or video hearing with contravener's representative + CA. Opportunity to present mitigating facts.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Compounding order</strong>: specifies compounding fee + payment timeline (usually 15 days).</li>"
         "<li><strong>Payment made &rarr; contravention compounded &rarr; closed</strong>: no further prosecution under FEMA.</li>"
         "</ul>"
         "<p><strong>Benefits of voluntary compounding</strong>:</p>"
         "<ul>"
         "<li>Materially lower fee than if caught during audit / scrutiny.</li>"
         "<li>Closes exposure permanently &mdash; no further prosecution.</li>"
         "<li>Avoids reputational damage.</li>"
         "<li>Required for M&amp;A / IPO / future FDI &mdash; without clean compounding history, future transactions may be blocked by AD bank.</li>"
         "</ul>"),
        ("/ Compounding fee structure", "What to budget for.",
         "<p>Compounding fee is at Compounding Authority's discretion but follows published guidelines (RBI Master Direction on Compounding):</p>"
         "<ul>"
         "<li><strong>Base fee</strong>: proportional to amount involved + nature of breach.</li>"
         "<li><strong>FC-GPR / FC-TRS late filing (minor delay)</strong>: typically INR 10,000-50,000 per instance.</li>"
         "<li><strong>FLA / APR non-filing (per year)</strong>: typically INR 10,000-50,000 per year.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Pricing guideline breach</strong>: higher &mdash; up to multiples of the pricing variance.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Sector cap breach / Press Note 3 breach</strong>: significant &mdash; can be 100%+ of amount involved; may require unwinding.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Multi-year / multi-instance breaches</strong>: compound; often grouped under single application for cost efficiency.</li>"
         "</ul>"
         "<p>Rough range for typical foreign-subsidiary cleanup: INR 50,000 - INR 10 lakh depending on scope and complexity. Material certainty achieved for small outlay.</p>"),
        ("/ Compounding application workflow", "90-180 day execution.",
         "<ol>"
         "<li><strong>Audit of FEMA compliance history</strong>: review all FC-GPR, FC-TRS, FLA, APR, Form ODI, ECB filings for 5+ year history. Identify breaches + severity.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Fact pattern + chronology</strong>: for each breach, draft chronology with dates, amounts, parties, delay period, reason (if any).</li>"
         "<li><strong>Supporting document collection</strong>: FIRC (Foreign Inward Remittance Certificate), bank statements, board resolutions, share certificates, AD bank correspondence.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Compounding application drafting</strong>: comprehensive application covering all identified breaches, admission of facts, mitigating circumstances, prayer for compounding.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Submission to Compounding Authority</strong>: via AD-I category bank. Along with application fee + supporting documents.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Acknowledgement</strong>: RBI acknowledges + schedules hearing (typically within 60-90 days).</li>"
         "<li><strong>Hearing</strong>: in-person or video hearing with CA representation. BQP partner attends. Specific questions on breach + mitigating facts.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Compounding order</strong>: within 180 days of acknowledgement. Specifies fee + payment deadline.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Fee payment</strong>: via RTGS to designated government account within 15 days.</li>"
         "<li><strong>Compliance certificate</strong>: Compounding Authority issues closure certificate &mdash; breach formally compounded. File copy forever.</li>"
         "</ol>"
         "<p><em>Last updated: 2026-10-14.</em></p>"),
    ],
    faqs=[
        ("What is FEMA compounding?",
         "Voluntary settlement mechanism under Section 13 of FEMA Act 1999 to regularise historical FEMA breaches. Contravener (Indian subsidiary / Indian company / Indian individual) files application with RBI Compounding Authority admitting the breach, pays compounding fee as determined, and the contravention is formally compounded - no further prosecution. Materially cheaper and more certain than being caught in audit / scrutiny."),
        ("When should an Indian subsidiary pursue voluntary compounding?",
         "When FEMA breaches are discovered - typically during pre-IPO due diligence, pre-M&A due diligence, auditor review, or internal compliance review. Voluntary compounding is cheaper than compounding driven by RBI inspection. Compounding should be pursued before any formal RBI notice because once a notice is issued, lower-fee voluntary route may close."),
        ("How much does FEMA compounding cost?",
         "Discretionary - set by Compounding Authority based on guidelines + amount + nature + delay. Rough ranges: FC-GPR/FC-TRS late filing INR 10,000-50,000 per instance; FLA / APR non-filing INR 10,000-50,000 per year; pricing guideline breach higher. Total cleanup for typical foreign-subsidiary: INR 50,000 - INR 10 lakh. BQP engagement fee separate."),
        ("What breaches are typically compounded for foreign-subsidiary companies?",
         "Most common: missed/late FC-GPR filing after inward FDI remittance; late FC-TRS on share transfer; non-filing of FLA Return (annual); non-filing of Form ODI Part II/APR for Indian company's overseas investment; pricing guideline breach on share issue to non-resident; delays in share allotment post remittance; Press Note 3 breach (investment from land-bordering country without approval)."),
        ("How long does FEMA compounding take?",
         "90-180 days from application submission to compounding order. Includes application review (30-60 days), hearing scheduling (another 30-60 days), hearing + order drafting (30-60 days). Fee payment within 15 days of order. Timing varies by Compounding Authority's docket + complexity of breach."),
        ("Does BQP handle FEMA compounding for foreign-subsidiary companies?",
         "Yes - this is a specialised service. Audit of FEMA compliance history + chronology drafting + supporting document assembly + compounding application preparation + submission + hearing representation + fee payment coordination + closure certificate. Scope-based engagement typically 2-6 months. WhatsApp +91 78018 87130."),
    ],
    related=[
        ("fla-return-foreign-liabilities-assets-rbi-india.html", "Guide", "FLA Return"),
        ("foreign-company-setup-indian-subsidiary-wos.html", "Guide", "WOS Setup"),
    ],
    cta_headline="FEMA breaches surfaced in diligence? Compound voluntarily.",
    cta_body="Materially cheaper than audit-driven compounding. Closes exposure permanently. 90-180 day timeline. BQP end-to-end: audit + application + hearing representation + closure. Scope-based engagement.",
))

